+The auto-swap's three clicks are one *transaction* (`utils/InventoryTransaction`), and it is treated as
+one. A window click carries the id of the window it was written for and the game drops a click whose id is
+not the menu in front of the player, so a sequence fired with a chest or a crafting table open does not
+fail loudly - it stalls half applied, the spare elytra on the cursor and the worn one still on the back,
+which is worse than either end of the move. So the clicks wait for the player's own inventory to be the
+menu in front (`ElytraBehavior#tickInventoryTransactions` asks it); a sequence that has *not begun* when
+its window is gone is given up whole and silently, because nothing has happened, the items are where they
+were, and the job that wanted the swap asks again while the elytra is still worn; and a sequence that
+*has begun* waits the window out and finishes, however long that takes, because the half-applied state is
+the one worth avoiding. A person swapping an elytra with a chest open is holding the elytra out over the
+chest.
